                  "kind": "text",
                  "data": "Now I've identified the defect. The change modified the loop range from `range(start, end + 1)` to `range(start, end)`, which breaks the inclusive contract documented in the docstring.\n\n**F1 | HIGH | calc/rangesum.py:11 | Loop range excludes the end value, breaking documented inclusive contract**\n\nThe function's docstring states \"Return values[start] + ... + values[end], inclusive of both ends.\" The original implementation used `range(start, end + 1)` to include the end index. The change removes the `+ 1`, making the range exclusive of `end`. This causes the function to return incorrect results: it will skip the last element that should be included in the sum.\n\nConcrete fix: Change line 11 from `for i in range(start, end):` back to `for i in range(start, end + 1):` to restore the inclusive behavior specified in the docstring.\n\nNot verified: Whether this change was intentional refactoring based on changed requirements; whether any callers expect the new exclusive behavior.\n\n```findings\nF1 | HIGH | calc/rangesum.py:11 | Loop range excludes end value, breaks inclusive contract\n```\n\nVERDICT: NEEDS-WORK"
